  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1)\n\nDisturbance rippling across the Great Lakes with seasonally strong\nnorthwesterly flow aloft will offer a severe storm threat this\nevening. Ongoing convection near Lake Michigan continues to grow\nupscale this afternoon and will likely head south-southeast as a\nmature MCS early this evening. With moderate instability (somewhat\ndampened by an elevated smoke layer across our area) and around 50 \nknots of effective bulk shear, there will be a increasing threat of \ndamaging winds accompanying the storms. It is likely the storm mode\nwill be somewhat linear by the systems arrival in our northwestern\ncounties, but can't rule out a brief large hail and spin-up/QLCS\ntornado threat as well. In addition, very heavy rain could cause\nlocalized flash flooding as well... especially in flood-prone areas.\n\nThe main uncertainty with this system is how far east the MCS\nreaches. The main instability gradient looks to be setting up near \nand west of the Interstate 75 corridor. If this holds, the highest\nthreat of damaging winds will coincide with this region. We will\ncontinue to monitor storm- and meso-scale evolution as the event\nunfolds.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1)\n\nA cold front will sweep southeast through our local area Tuesday\nmorning. Can't rule out a few showers or an isolated storm along the\nfront, but severe threat looks to be low at this time. Northwesterly\nwinds behind the front will slowly bring slightly cooler and drier\nair lasting through most of the week. Temperatures will be a bit\nbelow normal through at least Thursday and potentially into Friday.",
